##########################
# General benchmarking methodology
# 
# Redis recommends about 25 GB of data per shard, to satisfy performance and high-availablility requirements. 
# Each shard can utilize at most REDISEARCH_WORKERS=16 parallel workers.
#
# Example 1: Saturate 128 vCPUs.  
# To saturate 128 vCPUs, we needs REDISEARCH_WORKERS=16 and 8 shards. 
# In addition, since we expect about 25GB of data per shard, we need about 200GB of data. 
# Since since we consume approximately 6GB of memory capacity per M vectors, we need at least 33M vectors. 
# The closest vector-db-bencnmark supports is 40M vectors. Thus, we can configure our benchmark with 
# 8 shards, 16 workers per shard and 40M vectors. 
#
# Example 2: Benchmark a large dataset with 200M vectors
# Since we consume approximately 6GB of memory capacity per M vectors, we can expect 200M vectors to consume about 1.2TB of data. 
# Since Redis recommends about 25GB per shard, we need to create 48 shards. 
# For best performance, we can utilize REDISEARCH_WORKERS=16 per shard. 
# Thus, we need 16 workers * 48 shards = 768 vCPUs. 
# In this case, we could utilize a cluster of 4 machines with 192 vCPUs each. 
#
# In all cases, when we compare performance results (RPS), we need to compare at the same precision. 
# When increasing the number of shards, precision will increase, since each shard responds with top K results. 
##########################


PYTHON_PATH=/usr/bin/python3
HOME_PATH=$HOME
VECTORDB_BENCHMARK_PATH=$HOME_PATH/vector-db-benchmark
VECTORDB_BENCHMARK_BRANCH=fix_parameters
# Virtual environment will be created at: $HOME_PATH/venv-redis-benchmark
SKIP_UPLOAD=0       # Set SKIP_UPLOAD to 1 to skip uploading the data -> run benchmark on existing redis instance with existing data. Make sure that the same dataset is selected in DATASET_SIZE!
SKIP_SETUP=0      # Set SKIP_SETUP to 1 to skip setup of redis

##########################
# Redis settings #
##########################

